  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Parametric: geometry driven by rules', note: 'A model built from relationships (this curve offsets that surface by f(sun angle)) instead of fixed lines: change a parameter, the whole building updates coherently. The drawing stops being an artefact and becomes a system (the software lesson\'s abstraction, spatialised).' },
    { math: '2 · The lineage', note: 'Gaudi\'s hanging-chain models (form-finding by gravity — the catenary lesson), Frei Otto\'s soap films, and the algorithmic turn: Gehry Technologies\' aerospace software, Zaha Hadid Architects\' mathematics. The tools were built for aeroplanes; architecture borrowed them.' },
    { math: '3 · Optimisation: the computer proposes', note: 'Generative design loops: constraints + objectives (daylight, area, cost) → algorithms generate and score thousands of options. Multi-objective optimisation returns a Pareto front of trade-offs (the Ashby lesson\'s charts, searched by machine) — the human chooses among good options instead of drawing one.' },
    { math: '4 · The honest critiques', note: '"Parametricism" as a style produced curves that were expensive to build and sometimes unloved; the tool is neutral (it optimises whatever you tell it — the AI-alignment lesson\'s specification problem, architectural edition). The mature practice: computation in service of performance and constructability, not spectacle.' },
  ],
