<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/expert/computational-and-parametric-design">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Computational design is <strong>architecture\'s software turn</strong>: buildings described as
    rule systems (parameters and relationships), machines exploring option spaces, and
    fabrication data flowing straight from model to robot. The pencil didn\'t disappear — it
    gained a compiler.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="rules, lineage, optimisation, critiques" widget="equationStep" config={paramCfg} />

  <h2>The practice today</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>BIM to digital twins:</strong> the model that coordinates construction (the drawing lesson\'s database) becomes a live twin operating the building (sensors feeding the model) — the control-systems lesson\'s feedback, running a hospital or campus.</li>
    <li><strong>Digital fabrication closes the loop:</strong> CNC cutting, robotic bricklaying, 3-D-printed concrete and steel — complexity becomes free at the machine (a curved panel costs no more than a flat one to cut), reversing an economics that shaped architecture for centuries.</li>
    <li><strong>Environmental simulation everywhere:</strong> the environmental-design lesson\'s daylight/energy tools, now embedded in the parametric loop — every generated option scored before it is drawn; performance stops being a post-design report.</li>
    <li><strong>AI in the studio:</strong> generative image models as concept sketchers, LLMs in spec-writing and code-compliance checking (the AI lesson\'s tooling) — the profession\'s next decade mirrors every other knowledge trade: the routine automated, the judgement human, the integration decisive.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Terzidis, Algorithmic Architecture; the Gaudi and Frei Otto form-finding literature; Iveson on generative design in practice (Autodesk research).</p>
</TopicPage>
